# Everything, in the order you reach for it

<PageMeta level="Reference" time="10 min" assumes="nothing" sprite="hash" />

<ThemeToggle />

This page is not a tutorial. It does not read front to back. Scan it when you know what you want
and cannot remember how it is spelled. Every entry links to the page that teaches it.

## The five calls

All five come from `@markless/core`. You call them inside a component body. The lines under each
one are the form, not a whole file. The page linked beside it has the file to copy.

```tsrx
import { computed, element, shared, state, storage } from '@markless/core';
```

`state(initial)` declares a variable the page follows. Read it and assign it exactly like an
ordinary variable. See [state](/markless/concepts/state).

```tsrx
let count = state(0);
count++;
```

`computed(fn)` declares a value worked out from other values. It recalculates when one of its
inputs changes. Give it an `async` body and it becomes an async value, which needs a
[`@try` boundary](/markless/concepts/async) to be read from markup. See
[computed](/markless/concepts/computed).

```tsrx
const total = computed(() => shirts * 20 + mugs * 8);
const feed = computed(async () => fetchFeed(url.href));
```

`storage(key, fallback)` works like `state`, and also saves the value in the browser so it survives
a reload. Both arguments must be static strings. Omit the key and one is derived from the variable
name. See [storage](/markless/build/storage).

```tsrx
let theme = storage('theme', 'system');
```

`element<T>()` creates a handle you bind to one node with `el`. Later code uses it to reach the real
DOM node. It reads `undefined` before the node exists and after it is gone, so read it with `?.`
inside a handler. See [elements](/markless/build/elements).

```tsrx
let field = element<HTMLInputElement>();
// <input el={field} />
// field?.focus();
```

`shared(fn)` declares data that components resolve by calling it. That replaces threading the value
down through props.

Read [shared](/markless/build/shared) before you use it. On the published version this site is built
on, there is a catch. A definition in one module and a reader in another stalls the production
build. Passing the value through props is the working advice today.

## The props every host element accepts

```txt
onClick={}   and every other on<Event> name: runs when this node fires that DOM event
el={}        binds one element() handle to this node
attach={}    installs longer-lived behaviour on this node, and may return a cleanup function
class={}     a string, including a state variable holding a string (see the caveat below)
style={}     a style string
```

**One caveat on `class={}`, and it is the site's own.** On published 0.3.1 the compiler writes no
DOM update for a `class` binding. A class that is an expression renders correctly from the server,
then never changes again. [Styling](/markless/concepts/styling) has the measurement and what to
write instead.

`attach` belongs on a host element, never on a component. It may return a cleanup function, which
runs when the element goes away. An array of behaviours installs in order and cleans up in reverse.

## The constructs

Control flow is part of the language. You write it with a leading `@` inside markup. These too are
forms rather than files, written inside a component body. Each one links to the page that runs it.

```tsrx
@if (open) { <p>Shown</p> } @else { <p>Hidden</p> }
```

```tsrx
@switch (kind) {
	@case 'alpha': { <p>A</p> }
	@case 'beta': { <p>B</p> }
	@default: { <p>Other</p> }
}
